<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Affranchi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as affranchie, affranchis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Affranchi was a French legal term for a freedman or emancipated slave, also used pejoratively for free people of color; in English it names the class of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> and other slave-holding French territories, whose legal rights stood between those of free whites and enslaved Africans. Affranchis could get some education and own land, and many owned slaves: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> planter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julien-raimond">Julien Raimond</a> claimed they held a third of the colony's slaves. Colonial laws barred them from voting, administrative posts and careers as doctors or lawyers. A 1791 court decision in their favour provoked a backlash from white planters that helped bring on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a>.</p></div>
